  "uuid": "a91e363c-3fdb-4305-b31a-30f2edab597d",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "36325fd4-a36f-90ec-9bae-8fb5a0321fe4",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "2d71bebf-7de6-9587-4ec8-8625627e350c",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "World Health Organization studies reveal that young people are as vulnerable to strokes as older people. Contrary to popular belief, an estimated 94 per cent of deaths from strokes in South Asia occur among people who are younger than age 70. In other cases, strokes may also cause life-long disabilities.  ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The National Health Survey of Pakistan (NHSP) estimates that 0.5 to 0.8 million people in Pakistan suffer from strokes and heart diseases. This is partially due to the fact that there is a lack of neurologists and appropriate medical equipment available to treat patients who have suffered from strokes stated Dr Mughis Sheerani, Associate Professor, Department of Medicine, AKUH,K. A survey of general practitioners in Pakistan reveals that many doctors use sedatives to control blood pressure among stroke patients, further worsening their condition. This occurs despite the availability of less damaging medical options that can be used for the management of high blood pressure.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Conditions that increase the risk of strokes include high blood pressure, diabetes, heart diseases, obesity and tobacco use. According to the NHSP, high blood pressure affects one in three adults over the age of 45 and approximately one in five of those over the age of 15, while 35 per cent adults over the age of 45 suffer from diabetes and heart diseases. The survey also reveals that 28 per cent of women and 22 per cent men suffer from obesity, while 12 per cent of women and 40 per cent men use tobacco. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Mohammad Wasay, Associate Professor, AKUH, said that according to WHO estimates, deaths due to strokes in Pakistan are as high as 84 per 100,000 individuals, as compared to less than 20 per 100,000 individuals in the USA and the UK. South Asia houses about 20 per cent of the global stroke population, and the projected increase in the occurrence of strokes and heart diseases in South Asia is expected to rise more than any other region in the world.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "There is a need for research, education, and development of national guidelines on stroke prevention in Pakistan. Collaboration to create national preventive programmes is necessary to combat the effects of this disease. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "..........................................................................................................................................",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
